 "answer_1_where_is_the_clamp": {
  "model.py": "stable_audio_3/model.py line 350-351 in StableAudioModel.generate(): `if not return_latents: result = result.to(torch.float32).clamp(-1, 1)`, after sample_diffusion(decode=not return_latents).",
  "makers_own_usage": "No scaling by the peak anywhere. The CLI (cli.py _save_output) saves generate()'s output with torchaudio.save; the Gradio interface (interface/diffusion_cond.py line ~232) does `audio.to(float32).clamp(-1,1).mul(32767).to(int16)`. So the adapter did not skip a peak-scaling step of their pipeline; the makers clamp too. The clamp is theirs, but it is the last step after the decode and can be bypassed.",
  "way_to_pre_clamp_audio": "generate(return_latents=True) is the makers' own switch: it returns the sampled latents (decode=False) and skips the clamp. Their decoder then decodes them: pretransform.decode(latents cast to the pretransform dtype, chunked=None), exactly the line sample_diffusion() runs (inference/sampling.py line 509-511). Not reproduced: the zeroing of the padding region after the decode (sampling.py 513-522), which lies beyond the asked duration and is removed by their truncation anyway; the acceptance test shows it makes no difference."
 },
 "answer_2_change": {
  "adapter": "tools/sound/adapters/sa3.py make(): generate(..., return_latents=True) under torch.no_grad, then pretransform.decode, float32, truncation to int(seconds*sample_rate). No scaling, no clamp. gen.json items now carry audio_from and peak_before_clamp; `peak` (written by common.Record) is the same measured value, since the file now is the unclamped audio.",
  "old_files_kept": "assets/audio/gen/sa3/raw_clamped/ (72 wav + copy of the old gen.json)",
  "new_files": "assets/audio/gen/sa3/raw/ (72 wav, float32, 44100 Hz, 2 ch), same prompts, seeds, settings (50 steps, cfg 7, euler, fp16, prefix); made through tools/voice/with_lock.py --wait 3600, about 1.6 to 2.5 s each.",
  "command": ".venv-audio/Scripts/python.exe tools/voice/with_lock.py --wait 3600 -- .venv-sound/sa3/Scripts/python.exe tools/sound/adapters/sa3.py"
 },
